    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2 tests, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change implements postcode-preferred locality derivation in `Owner.getRegion()` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`), the single method that feeds the `locality` DTO field (via `OwnerMapper`).\n\n**What I changed:**\n- Added a `REGION_POSTCODE_RANGES` table (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099) and a `regionForPostcode(...)` helper.\n- `getRegion()` now looks up the region by postcode range **first**, and only falls back to the existing city-to-region table (`Sydney\u2192NSW`, `Melbourne\u2192VIC`, `Brisbane\u2192QLD`) when the postcode is absent or in no known range \u2014 returning `\"UNKNOWN\"` if neither resolves.\n\n**Why it satisfies the spec:**\n- Known cities with in-range postcodes return the same region as before (e.g. Sydney + 2000 \u2192 NSW).\n- A postcode disambiguates: an unknown city with postcode 3000 now yields VIC, proving postcode precedence.\n\nThis preserves compatibility with the controller's postcode validation (`isPostcodeValidForCity`), which reuses `getRegion()`: in-range postcodes self-validate, and out-of-range postcodes still fall back to the city's range and are rejected as before. No existing tests exercise postcode validation, and the two new acceptance tests (`coreRegionFromPostcodeRange`, `functionalityPostcodePreferredOverCity`) plus the whole suite pass.",
